              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent inpatient stay of your patient, a 58-year-old male, admitted on 09/04/1992 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically suspicious for appendicitis. Blood tests revealed elevated inflammatory markers (leukocytosis, elevated CRP). Abdominal ultrasound confirmed the diagnosis of acute appendicitis without evidence of perforation. The patient underwent laparoscopic appendectomy under general anaesthesia on 10/04/1992 without intraoperative complications. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Adequate analgesia and intravenous fluid replacement were administered throughout the hospital stay. Wound care was performed regularly. Vital signs remained stable throughout. The patient was mobilised early with physiotherapy support and discharged home on 14/04/1992 in good general condition. Please arrange follow-up in approximately two weeks. Do not hesitate to contact me with any questions.</div>"
            },
